# Development Concept Plan: Guinn Woods Administrative Site, Gettysburg National Military Park (National Park Service, Denver Service Center; recommended March 8, 1989, approved 1989)
# A planning document of 1989; only its history paragraph (PDF p. 11, printed p. 6) is extracted: the Guinn farm behind the Union line, its buildings used as temporary field hospitals, the site's farm and wood in 1863.
# The text layer is a clean machine text with apostrophe damage; quotes are anchors cut from the page.
@ NPS-DCP-GUINN

11/6 | buildings | The Guinn farm: 150 acres owned by the William Guinn family from 1776 to 1876; the 72-acre eastern portion kept by the Guinn daughters | - | pre-1863 to 1876 (ownership of the farm) | stated | The plan says the Guinn Woods site was historically part of a larger 150-acre farm owned by the William Guinn family from 1776 to 1876, whose western parcel west of Taneytown Road was gradually sold off while the 72-acre eastern portion including the site was kept by the surviving Guinn daughters until 1876 | #s1-2
11/6 | hospitals | The Guinn farm buildings, north of the present Hunt Avenue, were used as temporary field hospitals by Union troops | - | 1863-07 | stated | The plan says the Guinn farm was behind the Union battle lines during the battle and that its buildings, just north of what is now Hunt Avenue, were used as temporary field hospitals by Union troops | #s3-4
11/6 | fields | The Guinn site in 1863: partly in agriculture, partly wooded, three streams; the western part used as a shortcut between the Taneytown Road and the Baltimore Pike | - | 1863-07 | stated | The plan says the portion of the farm south of the farm buildings was not used by the Union forces for any specific purpose other than troop movements and obtaining water and shade, that at the time of the fighting the site was partly in agriculture and partly wooded and had three streams, and that the westernmost agricultural section was a shortcut between the Taneytown Road and the Baltimore Pike for Union troops moving to and from Culp's Hill and the Cemetery Ridge line on July 2 and 3 | #s5-7
11/6 | general | The rest of the Guinn tract most likely sheltered unattached soldiers and staff officers at various times | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The plan says that most likely the rest of the tract sheltered unattached soldiers and staff officers at various times during the battle | #s8
11/6 | woodlots | The open meadow sections of the Guinn site grew up into woods after farming ended in the 1920s and 1930s | - | ~1920s-1930s (growth after farming ended; not a state of 1863) | stated | The plan says the site remained in farmland until the 1920s and 1930s and that the historically open meadow sections grew up into woods during that time, extending the vegetation line up to the residences and to Hunt Avenue | #s9-10
